WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.968
- OK, so if business is a sport—
- Yep.

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.837
- Tell me your NBA comparison.

00:00:03.837 --> 00:00:06.406
Who are you in the sport of business?

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.942
- I'm Wilt. I'm Jordan.

00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:10.877
I'm LeBron. I'm Luka.
- [Laughing]

00:00:10.877 --> 00:00:13.680
- I'm Dirk. I'm Magic. I'm Bird, all rolled into one.
- You're all of it, yeah.

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:17.117
- Rolled into one.
- All into one!

00:00:17.617 --> 00:00:41.775
- All into one! You can't touch me!
- I love that.

00:00:41.775 --> 00:00:42.842
The man himself!
- What's up?

00:00:42.842 --> 00:00:45.445
- What's up, Mark? How you doin'?

00:00:45.712 --> 00:00:46.446
- I'm great. How are you, Taylor?

00:00:46.446 --> 00:00:47.280
- I'm so good.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:51.084
You know, normally I would say, "Welcome,"
but we're doing this in your domain.

00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:51.718
- Welcome!

00:00:51.718 --> 00:00:53.153
- Yeah, I'm so happy to be here.

00:00:53.153 --> 00:00:55.555
So this is, what, admin, sales?
- Yeah,

00:00:55.588 --> 00:00:56.656
so, there's two sides.

00:00:56.656 --> 00:00:58.792
This is the back office side.
- OK.

00:00:58.792 --> 00:01:02.062
- And then on the other side is the practice
facility and the front office side.

00:01:02.195 --> 00:01:03.730
- Yeah, no, it's so beautiful.

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:07.333
I was just saying to Jackie, I was like,
it looks like a startup. Like, the layout of it.
- Yeah, it does.

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:08.368
- It's very open.
- That's the whole idea.

00:01:08.368 --> 00:01:10.570
- You did that on purpose?
- Yeah, I do it for all my companies.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:13.106
- Yeah.
- It's not like real fancy or anything.

00:01:13.106 --> 00:01:16.042
You know, I'm not big on
spending money on furniture or offices.

00:01:16.042 --> 00:01:17.077
- Right.
- I'd rather put it on the court.

00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:19.646
- Yeah, no, it's cool.
- Yeah.
- It's very hip in here.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.615
OK, so the last time I saw you—I saw you,

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:25.085
but you didn't see me. It was after,
I think, Game 5 of

00:01:25.085 --> 00:01:26.086
Western Conference Finals.

00:01:26.086 --> 00:01:27.387
And it was like, you,

00:01:27.387 --> 00:01:31.691
Dirk, Luka, Chet Holmgren, all talking in the hallway.
- Oh, after the game, yeah.

00:01:31.691 --> 00:01:32.959
- Yes. Everyone's snapping pics.

00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:36.663
What do you remember about that
conversation with all those guys?

00:01:36.663 --> 00:01:39.165
- Just trying to come down
from being pissed off. [Laughing]

00:01:39.165 --> 00:01:41.935
- Yeah, yeah, I'm sure.
- You know,

00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:45.872
but yeah, I mean, seeing Dirk
and Luka is always fun.

00:01:45.872 --> 00:01:49.476
Met Chet for the first time, and, you know,
any kid—that was predraft,

00:01:49.476 --> 00:01:50.243
so any kid coming in

00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:53.913
is always excited and—
- Yeah.
- Obviously, you know, him meeting Dirk and Luka,

00:01:53.913 --> 00:01:54.981
he didn't care about me.

00:01:54.981 --> 00:01:57.650
He didn't even know I was there.
- [Laughing] Yeah. He's like, "I just wanna meet these guys."

00:01:57.684 --> 00:01:58.251
- These guys, yeah.

00:01:58.251 --> 00:02:01.121
- So when I see Luka and Dirk,
and give me your thought on this,

00:02:01.154 --> 00:02:03.256
cuz I go back and forth on it all the time.

00:02:03.256 --> 00:02:07.694
I'm like, is it unfair the way that, like, fans
and the media

00:02:07.694 --> 00:02:11.631
almost put on Luka, like, "Well,
you have to be what Dirk was.

00:02:11.764 --> 00:02:14.400
That's what you have to
mean to the Mavericks."

00:02:14.701 --> 00:02:17.070
- Yeah, it is a little bit unfair,
but the good news is

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.772
they've got similar personalities
in a lot of respects.

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:22.742
If you knew Dirk when he was 21, 22, 23,

00:02:23.109 --> 00:02:27.714
you would not—like, if they looked the same
and you would just judge their actions—

00:02:27.714 --> 00:02:30.950
- Yeah.
- They're a lot the same
- In what ways?
- Just their attitude

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:33.052
towards life,
having fun, focus on the game.

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:35.388
So, work hard, play hard basically.
- Yeah.

00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:38.191
- But they kinda pick their spots.
And, you know,

00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:41.394
Dirk, it was a little bit more difficult
because he had more challenges

00:02:41.394 --> 00:02:43.196
with the language than Luka does.

00:02:43.196 --> 00:02:47.066
Luka speaks, like, 30 languages.
But they're so similar in

00:02:47.066 --> 00:02:49.235
so many respects.
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:02:49.235 --> 00:02:53.072
And I also think it seems like Dirk
genuinely is like a mentor to Luka.

00:02:53.173 --> 00:02:54.707
- Oh, yeah, without question.
- Yeah.

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:56.342
- I mean, they love each other.
They're close. I mean—

00:02:56.342 --> 00:02:59.312
- Yeah.
- You know, I've been on
some of the text chains. They're insane,

00:02:59.312 --> 00:03:02.849
first of all,
but they really do care for each other.

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:05.251
It's not like, "Oh, the cameras are on.
Let me give you a big hug."

00:03:05.518 --> 00:03:07.687
- Mmhmm.
- They'll spend time together. They'll get together here.

00:03:07.687 --> 00:03:10.023
They'll get together in Europe.
They'll get together wherever they can.

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:12.425
They're just two good guys hanging out.

00:03:12.425 --> 00:03:13.026
- Yeah.

00:03:13.026 --> 00:03:16.863
So when I spoke to you after the bubble,
we were talking about Luka

00:03:16.863 --> 00:03:17.797
for a little bit.
- Mmhmm.

00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:20.800
- And you said that you had asked him, "OK,
what are you gonna add to your game

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:21.434
this offseason?"

00:03:21.434 --> 00:03:23.803
And at that time, it was that he wanted a left hand—
- Right, his left, yeah.

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:27.173
- And wanted to be able to finish.
- Yeah.
- And that was what he added in the bubble season.

00:03:27.507 --> 00:03:31.010
- Without question, yeah.
- So what have—have you asked him that question yet?

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:33.012
- Yep, but I can't tell you.
- What?!

00:03:33.012 --> 00:03:36.482
Why?! You told me last time!
- No, I know! Cuz, I can't tell you.

00:03:36.783 --> 00:03:41.721
- OK.
- But, yeah, it was, it was crazy what
we talked about adding, and so we'll see.

00:03:41.721 --> 00:03:43.223
- OK.
- I don't want to, I don't want to jinx him.

00:03:43.223 --> 00:03:45.525
But if you see something and you're going,
"Where the hell did that come from?"

00:03:45.792 --> 00:03:47.093
You'll know that's what it was.

00:03:47.093 --> 00:03:47.293
- OK,

00:03:47.293 --> 00:03:50.296
just tell me, like, is it a move—?
- Yeah.
- Is it just a—OK.

00:03:50.296 --> 00:03:50.930
- It's a move. It's a shot.

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:55.435
- OK. So what would you want him to add?
- That shot,

00:03:55.435 --> 00:03:56.469
because it's insane.

00:03:56.469 --> 00:04:01.541
So two things that, you know,
the move that will not be disclosed.

00:04:02.075 --> 00:04:04.277
And I think, I think he's gonna learn
how to use the backboard.

00:04:04.277 --> 00:04:07.513
I think that's gonna be a thing
in the NBA where, you know, you see guys

00:04:07.513 --> 00:04:10.416
who are athletic throwing the ball off
the backboard and finishing with the dunk.

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:14.220
But I think strategically,
you're gonna see it added into the NBA,

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:16.889
and I think Luka's gonna benefit,
hopefully will benefit.

00:04:16.889 --> 00:04:19.859
So that's kinda the weird stuff
I talk to him about.

00:04:19.859 --> 00:04:20.159
- Yeah.

00:04:20.159 --> 00:04:22.996
- Like, you know,
it's like stuff that is so bizarre

00:04:22.996 --> 00:04:24.831
that nobody else would really consider
or do it.

00:04:24.831 --> 00:04:26.432
- Yeah.
- But that's the stuff I like to talk about.

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:28.468
- OK, give me another example
of the weird stuff.

00:04:28.468 --> 00:04:32.372
- No, I mean, that's as weird as it gets.
Like, "Luka, if you're down in the paint

00:04:32.639 --> 00:04:35.608
and you don't have what you want and
you're doing all your stuff, just toss it up

00:04:35.608 --> 00:04:39.512
against the backboard. Get it back,
reset the three-second clock and go back to work, you know?

00:04:39.812 --> 00:04:40.280
- Yeah.

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:42.849
- And, you know, he kinda rolls his eyes,
but we'll see what happens.

00:04:42.849 --> 00:04:45.451
- Yeah. I mean, I love that
that's your offseason question

00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:49.122
to Luka. It's like, "What are we going to see
you add next season?" Cuz you said,

00:04:49.122 --> 00:04:52.759
you know, the greats every season—
- Every year.
- They come back with something new.

00:04:52.759 --> 00:04:53.192
- Dirk would,

00:04:53.192 --> 00:04:55.094
you know, we always had that conversation,

00:04:55.094 --> 00:04:58.231
and Dirk wouldn't tell me. He would just show
me, you know? He said, "Just watch.

00:04:58.231 --> 00:04:58.998
I'm working."

00:04:58.998 --> 00:05:01.701
You know, everybody
that's ever been really, really good

00:05:02.702 --> 00:05:05.071
that we've had here
has always come back with something more.

00:05:05.071 --> 00:05:07.206
And that's what makes him great.
Luka hasn't hit his ceiling yet.

00:05:07.206 --> 00:05:09.208
Not even close.
- Yeah. So what is that?

00:05:09.208 --> 00:05:10.777
What is that Luka ceiling?

00:05:10.777 --> 00:05:13.613
- I mean, you know, he keeps on

00:05:13.646 --> 00:05:16.082
getting a stronger
and stronger feel for the game

00:05:16.949 --> 00:05:19.686
and his teammates
and integrating it all together.

00:05:19.952 --> 00:05:21.354
You know, there's one thing to be great and

00:05:21.354 --> 00:05:23.489
put up points, assists, rebounds, etc.

00:05:23.823 --> 00:05:27.694
It's a whole 'nother thing to understand
the time and place

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:31.664
during any game to know
what needs to happen with your teammates.

00:05:31.864 --> 00:05:36.402
And I still, I think he's good at it, but
he's on his way to becoming great at it.

00:05:36.736 --> 00:05:39.038
- Mmhmm.
- There aren't a lotta guys in this league that

00:05:39.706 --> 00:05:43.743
can lift their teammates
to a whole 'nother complete level.

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:47.380
Lots of guys can take over a game, but
there aren't a lotta guys that can help

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:51.184
their teammates take over a game or pick
which teammate will take over a game,

00:05:51.384 --> 00:05:54.921
or which combination of teammates will take
over a game on both sides of the ball.

00:05:55.221 --> 00:05:57.490
And Luka's got that unique ability.

00:05:57.490 --> 00:06:00.426
- What game this past season
did you see that the most?

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:02.595
- Phoenix Game 7.

00:06:02.595 --> 00:06:03.896
- Yeahhh!
- I mean, that was just—

00:06:05.064 --> 00:06:08.368
I'm, you know, it was just like,
"Don't pinch me," you know? It was just,

00:06:08.701 --> 00:06:11.771
you know, where that first half,
it was his game.

00:06:12.138 --> 00:06:15.541
And then the second half it was, OK,
let's let our guys do their thing and

00:06:15.742 --> 00:06:19.645
and have their role and amplify their role
and make them better.

00:06:19.779 --> 00:06:20.179
- Yeah.

00:06:20.179 --> 00:06:20.780
- You know,

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:24.450
people underestimate the value of a player
who can make his teammates better.

00:06:24.851 --> 00:06:26.352
You know, I think LeBron's like that.

00:06:26.352 --> 00:06:28.521
But there aren't aren't many
where you just say,

00:06:29.255 --> 00:06:31.991
you know, it doesn't really matter
who you put around him.

00:06:31.991 --> 00:06:32.959
Dirk was like that.

00:06:32.959 --> 00:06:33.626
You know,

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:36.496
a lotta people said prior
to our championship season,

00:06:36.496 --> 00:06:38.664
you know,
there was Dirk and Manny, Moe and Jack,

00:06:38.664 --> 00:06:43.202
you know? And we kept on winning 50 games
and making the playoffs.

00:06:43.202 --> 00:06:48.107
And so it takes a really special NBA
player to be able to make everybody around

00:06:48.107 --> 00:06:48.541
you better.

00:06:48.541 --> 00:06:50.576
- Yeah. I mean, and when you talk about that series,

00:06:50.576 --> 00:06:51.511
obviously a lotta discourse

00:06:51.511 --> 00:06:55.348
right now about the fact that you all
are gonna match up again next season.
- Yep.

00:06:55.348 --> 00:07:00.052
- But what did you learn about
Luka and Devin Booker in that series?

00:07:00.052 --> 00:07:01.154
- I mean, I'm not gonna talk about Book.

00:07:01.154 --> 00:07:03.423
You know, it's tough when,
you know, we've been on both sides.

00:07:03.423 --> 00:07:04.590
We lost in the conference finals.
- Yeah.

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:08.528
- So, you know, he's a great player,
and he'll do great things.

00:07:08.961 --> 00:07:13.566
But, you know, Luka's got that eye of the
Luka. I'm not even gonna say eye of the tiger,

00:07:13.566 --> 00:07:17.904
but he just gets that,
you know, that look in his eye where,

00:07:18.337 --> 00:07:21.340
"OK, this is what I'm gonna do."
- Mmhmm.
- You know, and whatever it takes.

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:24.110
And he just reads the game so well,
he doesn't have—

00:07:24.177 --> 00:07:27.313
you know, some guys have two spots
on the court, right?

00:07:27.313 --> 00:07:29.348
Or they have one thing
that they'll do all the time,

00:07:29.348 --> 00:07:31.384
or they'll get to the left
and they'll get a shot.

00:07:31.384 --> 00:07:33.753
Luka could be anywhere, anyhow.
You don't know what he's gonna do.

00:07:33.753 --> 00:07:35.955
Now, he likes the step-back three-pointer,

00:07:35.955 --> 00:07:39.292
but he can be anywhere on the court
and he's gonna be dangerous.

00:07:39.559 --> 00:07:42.361
- Can you always, like,
see when that look comes from him?

00:07:42.395 --> 00:07:44.130
- Well, it depends on the game, right?
- Yeah.

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:46.933
- Because there's some games where it's hard,
and, you know,

00:07:47.667 --> 00:07:51.404
people don't realize the mental challenge
to play 82 games,

00:07:52.004 --> 00:07:56.142
that, you know, you're not gonna
get up at the same level for every game.

00:07:56.142 --> 00:08:01.280
You're gonna play hard, you know, but it's—you
know, guys don't always respect

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:04.083
every team at the same level
or every player at the same level.

00:08:04.517 --> 00:08:07.653
And so when Luka really feels
that there's a challenge,

00:08:08.221 --> 00:08:10.590
I know, you know, he's goin' to work.
- Yeah.

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:11.757
- And when he doesn't feel

00:08:11.757 --> 00:08:14.794
there's as much of a challenge, you know,
he'll—that's games where

00:08:14.794 --> 00:08:18.030
he'll like, OK, go to his teammates and say,
"This is your chance," right?

00:08:18.064 --> 00:08:19.365
- Mmhmm.
- "We're gonna let you shine, Doe-Doe [Dorian Finney-Smith].

00:08:19.365 --> 00:08:22.101
We're gonna let you shine, Maxi [Kleber]." Whoever
it may be.

00:08:22.101 --> 00:08:22.702
- Yeah.

00:08:22.702 --> 00:08:23.870
So I think you may have touched

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:26.539
on the answer for this a bit,
but I wanna get your perspective on it.

00:08:26.639 --> 00:08:28.674
We talk a lot about what's a star.

00:08:28.674 --> 00:08:32.979
We talk about what's a superstar.
- Yeah.
- To you, what is the difference, and where is Luka

00:08:32.979 --> 00:08:36.115
right now?
- "Star" is somebody who can help the team win."

00:08:36.716 --> 00:08:38.818
A superstar is somebody
who can will his team to win.

00:08:39.785 --> 00:08:41.187
- Mmhmm.

00:08:41.187 --> 00:08:44.991
- You know, when it's not working,
you find there's

00:08:45.057 --> 00:08:48.127
a superstar will find a way to win
the game on either side of the court.

00:08:48.661 --> 00:08:51.597
You know,
there are a lot of great players.

00:08:51.597 --> 00:08:53.332
There are a lot of stars.

00:08:53.332 --> 00:08:56.435
But come crunch time,
they might not want the ball.

00:08:56.435 --> 00:08:57.837
They might not even be able
to get a shot off.

00:08:57.837 --> 00:08:59.672
And that's where the league
has changed a lot.

00:08:59.672 --> 00:09:03.242
And that's one of the reasons
we were so excited to trade to draft Luka,

00:09:03.543 --> 00:09:07.580
because in my—in our—opinion,
the best players

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:11.117
are the ones that can bring the ball up
the court and impact the game

00:09:11.117 --> 00:09:13.653
however they need to. Passing, shooting,
rebounding, whatever.

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:16.155
Whereas with other players
who may be stars,

00:09:16.355 --> 00:09:19.125
they need somebody else
to get them to their their spots

00:09:19.325 --> 00:09:21.727
or they need somebody
to get them the ball.

00:09:21.994 --> 00:09:25.531
And in this day and age,
with all the spacing that we have,

00:09:26.265 --> 00:09:29.402
you know, when that person
is the same person, right?

00:09:30.603 --> 00:09:31.771
They can be unstoppable.

00:09:31.771 --> 00:09:34.206
- Yeah. And where do you feel like
Luka is at this moment?

00:09:34.206 --> 00:09:35.908
How does he make the jump?

00:09:35.942 --> 00:09:38.311
- I think he's there, you know,
and it's not about making the jump.

00:09:38.311 --> 00:09:39.812
He is a superstar, right?
- Mmhmm.

00:09:39.812 --> 00:09:42.615
- It's a question of, you know,
can he be one of the all time greats?

00:09:43.049 --> 00:09:46.352
And I think he's showing everybody
that he can,

00:09:46.352 --> 00:09:48.054
and he is,

00:09:48.054 --> 00:09:50.256
and it's just a question of how good.
- Yeah.

00:09:50.256 --> 00:09:51.390
So for you, I mean, obviously,

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:55.294
this is a large part of the job for you
when you talk about what do we need to do

00:09:55.294 --> 00:09:59.198
to get this next championship,
how do all these things fit?
- Sure, sure.

00:09:59.198 --> 00:10:03.336
- Does Luka right now have all the pieces
around him to win a championship?

00:10:03.336 --> 00:10:03.536
- Yeah.

00:10:03.536 --> 00:10:04.737
I mean, if we you know,

00:10:04.737 --> 00:10:07.773
when we lost in the conference finals,
I don't think it was for lack of talent.

00:10:08.074 --> 00:10:08.608
I think

00:10:08.608 --> 00:10:12.311
the Warriors deserve a lot of credit
because they had played together so long.

00:10:12.545 --> 00:10:15.114
Their execution was phenomenal, right?
- Yeah.

00:10:15.114 --> 00:10:18.017
- The way they ran their offense,
knowing exactly where the ball was going,

00:10:18.017 --> 00:10:22.555
the way they had their rotations
defensively, you know, knowing who to get,

00:10:22.622 --> 00:10:27.093
who to push where and how to prevent
them, prevent guys from being comfortable.

00:10:27.426 --> 00:10:31.397
That wasn't as much talent
as it was corporate knowledge.
- Mmhmm.

00:10:31.797 --> 00:10:35.201
- You know, the experience of having played
together for all those years

00:10:35.201 --> 00:10:38.971
and been in crunch situations, knowing
what to do. We hadn't been there yet,

00:10:39.271 --> 00:10:43.175
you know? And so, we had been
out of the first round in 10 years.

00:10:43.175 --> 00:10:46.612
- Yeah.
- And so a lot of it was execution.

00:10:46.612 --> 00:10:48.648
And when you're talking to our guys
during the series,

00:10:48.914 --> 00:10:51.384
that was the thing that kept on
coming up, right?
- Yeah.

00:10:51.384 --> 00:10:53.753
- The Warriors knew
where to be on both sides of the ball,

00:10:54.186 --> 00:10:57.223
no matter how we adjusted,
cuz the teams we had played before hadn't

00:10:57.657 --> 00:10:59.258
had been as good at adjustments.

00:10:59.258 --> 00:11:04.230
And so I think for us it's not so much
we need that second star or whatever.

00:11:04.764 --> 00:11:08.234
It's more let's just get some time,
you know, and experience in crunch

00:11:08.234 --> 00:11:11.203
time situations in the playoffs
and that'll pay off.

00:11:11.437 --> 00:11:12.772
- But see, that's the thing. I agree.

00:11:12.772 --> 00:11:16.542
I think, number one, people
undervalue experience and they're not—

00:11:16.542 --> 00:11:18.544
but it's almost like a chicken
and an egg thing,

00:11:18.544 --> 00:11:22.815
because to get the experience,
You gotta get the experience.
- Yeah, of course. And it's hard.

00:11:22.848 --> 00:11:25.351
- So it's like, how do you simulate that?
- No, you can't simulate it.

00:11:25.351 --> 00:11:26.318
You gotta be there, right?

00:11:26.318 --> 00:11:27.420
I mean, if you look at the Celtics

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:30.856
Warriors in the Finals, you could see that
the Celtics had great talent,

00:11:31.190 --> 00:11:33.993
but the Warriors just outexecuted them,
the same thing they did to us.

00:11:33.993 --> 00:11:36.362
They didn't shoot them,
you know. They didn't,

00:11:36.362 --> 00:11:37.797
but they went with the guy,

00:11:37.797 --> 00:11:40.466
they got the guy who was hot, open,
got their looks.

00:11:40.466 --> 00:11:43.536
And, you know, it was just guys

00:11:43.536 --> 00:11:46.272
who had knew their roles,
like an Andrew Wiggins, right?

00:11:46.572 --> 00:11:48.174
I think he was the one who beat us.

00:11:48.174 --> 00:11:50.676
And I told him that after the series,
you know? It's like,

00:11:51.043 --> 00:11:56.115
we knew what to expect from Klay
and from Steph and from Draymond,

00:11:56.115 --> 00:12:01.087
you know, we didn't know what to expect
or how Wiggs would step up, and he did.

00:12:01.087 --> 00:12:03.989
- Yeah.
- And that's what it comes down to
in that type of series.

00:12:04.523 --> 00:12:07.693
The team understanding, everybody
understanding the roles,

00:12:07.960 --> 00:12:10.863
being able to execute
on what the coach puts out there for you

00:12:11.130 --> 00:12:15.468
and having guys you might call "role
players" step up when the time is right.

00:12:15.468 --> 00:12:18.003
- Yeah, I love that.
How did Andrew react when you told him?

00:12:18.237 --> 00:12:20.673
- He said, "Thank you, man."
You know, and then he went to celebrate.

00:12:20.673 --> 00:12:23.509
We're going to the Finals.
- [Laughs] You're like, "I don't care.

00:12:23.542 --> 00:12:25.478
Yeah, I know. We won."
And he just moves on.

00:12:25.478 --> 00:12:26.545
- Yeah.
- OK

00:12:26.545 --> 00:12:30.382
well, so speaking of championships,
obviously Dirk, such a cornerstone here.

00:12:30.583 --> 00:12:33.252
I read he's gonna have an
office somewhere.

00:12:33.252 --> 00:12:35.955
- Yeah, it's back on the other side.
Yeah, he does have an office.
- Yeah.

00:12:35.955 --> 00:12:38.758
- So does the office come with,
like, an expanded role?

00:12:38.758 --> 00:12:40.926
Is he gonna be like, very involved?
- Whatever he wants.
- Whatever he wants.

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:44.029
- Dirk is you know, he does
whatever he wants.

00:12:44.029 --> 00:12:46.532
- Totally.
- Yeah. It's not like, "Dirk,
what do you wanna do?"

00:12:46.532 --> 00:12:49.201
It's just like, "OK, do what you wanna do."
- Yeah.

00:12:49.201 --> 00:12:49.468
- Yeah.

00:12:49.468 --> 00:12:53.005
He's, I mean, he's a legacy, you know,
he knows the door is open.

00:12:53.005 --> 00:12:54.039
We talk all the time.

00:12:54.039 --> 00:12:57.943
So it's just, you know, after being,
you know, on the road

00:12:57.943 --> 00:13:01.380
for 21 years, you want to spend some time
with your family and all that.
- Yeah.

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:03.582
- So he he'll pick his time
when the time is right.

00:13:03.582 --> 00:13:06.519
But we talk and he's not shy
about sharing his opinion.

00:13:06.519 --> 00:13:08.354
- Yeah. He's like, "This is what I want."

00:13:08.354 --> 00:13:11.323
- And I think you said he's
just been bugging you about the statue.

00:13:11.323 --> 00:13:12.124
"When is it coming? When's the unveiling?"

00:13:12.124 --> 00:13:14.326
- It's coming. It's coming this year.
- OK.

00:13:14.426 --> 00:13:16.595
It is absolutely coming this year.
- Absolutely coming this year.

00:13:16.595 --> 00:13:19.765
- So a conversation we were having
actually is like, what do you think

00:13:19.765 --> 00:13:23.269
is the criteria
to get a statue at a place?

00:13:23.736 --> 00:13:25.171
- You have to stay there.

00:13:25.171 --> 00:13:26.071
It's about being there,

00:13:26.071 --> 00:13:28.607
about being a superstar,
leading the team to a championship

00:13:28.941 --> 00:13:31.110
and being there
for an extended period of time.

00:13:31.110 --> 00:13:32.511
- Yes.
- You know,

00:13:32.511 --> 00:13:37.049
that's key, because you know, it's
not just about what you do on the court.

00:13:37.049 --> 00:13:38.551
It's about what you do in the community.

00:13:38.551 --> 00:13:41.187
It's about being part of the
fabric of an organization.

00:13:42.221 --> 00:13:42.788
During and

00:13:42.788 --> 00:13:46.091
after your playing time, and
Dirk exemplifies that.

00:13:46.158 --> 00:13:47.026
- Absolutely. OK,

00:13:47.026 --> 00:13:50.763
give me to other players around the league
that you believe will have statues

00:13:50.763 --> 00:13:52.331
at their respective arenas.

00:13:52.331 --> 00:13:56.135
- I mean, LeBron obviously, and Steph.

00:13:56.702 --> 00:13:59.205
- Deservedly so.
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:13:59.205 --> 00:14:03.209
And I was actually just talking about this,
I know I asked for NBA, but I'm like,

00:14:03.576 --> 00:14:07.479
the Storm have to give Sue a statue.
- Yeah, of course. How long has he been there?

00:14:07.479 --> 00:14:13.018
Twenty years?
- Yeah, I'm like this is, she
is the Seattle Storm.
- She's the WNBA.

00:14:13.018 --> 00:14:15.054
- Yeah, and the thing with statues though,
I'm like, OK,

00:14:15.054 --> 00:14:17.056
they have to look exactly like the person.

00:14:17.056 --> 00:14:20.526
That would make me nervous!
- No it's not gonna work that way.
It's not ever gonna work that way.

00:14:20.593 --> 00:14:21.794
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:14:21.794 --> 00:14:22.061
OK,

00:14:22.061 --> 00:14:25.831
So I wanna talk a little bit about
Jalen Brunson, because I know you said

00:14:25.831 --> 00:14:28.634
obviously there's no hard feelings
between you guys, you're excited for him.

00:14:29.101 --> 00:14:32.071
But I have a lot of players on this show
that talk about

00:14:32.071 --> 00:14:34.874
what it's like to be traded away
or like it being a business.

00:14:35.107 --> 00:14:36.842
I don't always have an owner.

00:14:36.842 --> 00:14:38.744
Tell me what that moment is like.

00:14:38.744 --> 00:14:41.847
So what emotion do you tie to

00:14:41.847 --> 00:14:44.183
Jalen Brunson deciding to not stay?

00:14:44.984 --> 00:14:47.820
- I mean, it's weird because
we kinda knew it was happening.

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:51.991
There was a whole confluence of issues
coming along after

00:14:52.024 --> 00:14:56.462
we got eliminated from the playoffs
and there's just hint after hint after hint.

00:14:56.462 --> 00:15:00.199
So by the time it happened, you know,
we knew he'd already broken up with us.

00:15:00.499 --> 00:15:03.002
And so it was just a matter
of who else he was gonna start dating.

00:15:03.335 --> 00:15:05.371
- Yeah.
- You know? And so, yeah.

00:15:05.838 --> 00:15:09.608
So it wasn't like, "Oh no, oh my goodness,
I can't believe this is happening."

00:15:09.608 --> 00:15:11.610
It was like, "OK, it's coming."
- Yeah.

00:15:11.610 --> 00:15:15.447
It's like you felt it.
- Yeah.
- When you say it was hint after hint after hint.

00:15:15.481 --> 00:15:16.382
What do you mean by that?

00:15:17.549 --> 00:15:22.688
- You know. Um, you

00:15:22.688 --> 00:15:26.091
know, it's just there are, you know,
you can just tell, that's all.

00:15:26.091 --> 00:15:28.260
There are certain moves
that other teams were making,

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:30.296
you know, to open up a cap room.

00:15:30.296 --> 00:15:33.565
And so that, you know, made it
kind of obvious where he was going.

00:15:33.599 --> 00:15:35.301
- Yeah.
- And that's the way it played out.
- Yeah.

00:15:35.301 --> 00:15:39.705
What kind of position does that put you in
when you feel it?

00:15:39.705 --> 00:15:41.607
- Um, look, it's
part of the game, you know. It's

00:15:41.607 --> 00:15:44.777
the same position is going into a trade
or going into the trade deadline.

00:15:45.144 --> 00:15:48.781
You know, players get nervous,
and rightfully so, because you just never

00:15:48.781 --> 00:15:49.448
know.

00:15:49.448 --> 00:15:52.151
You know, having been through 20-plus
trade deadlines now,

00:15:52.651 --> 00:15:56.155
99.99 percent of proposed trades don't happen.

00:15:56.522 --> 00:16:01.060
And 95 percent of the ones that you think
are going to happen still don't happen.

00:16:01.460 --> 00:16:04.763
But in this day and age of social media,
everything gets reported

00:16:04.763 --> 00:16:06.398
as if it's a done deal.
- Yeah.

00:16:06.398 --> 00:16:11.036
- And so it's no different than players,
you know, feeling that those butterflies

00:16:11.036 --> 00:16:15.007
and everything, you know,
you don't want it to happen,

00:16:15.007 --> 00:16:16.508
like with JB leaving, but

00:16:17.509 --> 00:16:20.012
you can't tell a player
it's just a business.

00:16:20.012 --> 00:16:21.480
And then when it happens to you say it's

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:24.350
not a business, it's on the player.
No, it goes both ways.

00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:25.818
That's the way it is
- Yeah, I would

00:16:25.818 --> 00:16:28.921
think, and correct me if I'm wrong,
you feel like and from all the things

00:16:28.921 --> 00:16:30.756
I've watched from you,
you're a competitive person.

00:16:30.756 --> 00:16:32.925
- Yeah, of course. Yeah.
- So you're like, "Well, I want to win."

00:16:32.958 --> 00:16:35.694
- Yeah.
- "I want this to be,
you know, all of these things."

00:16:35.694 --> 00:16:39.765
So I'm just curious for you,
is it easy for you to, like, let go of

00:16:39.765 --> 00:16:42.501
when something doesn't go
your way? Do you, like, sit on it?

00:16:42.701 --> 00:16:43.135
- Depends on what it is.

00:16:43.135 --> 00:16:45.871
- So there's certain things I have control
over. If I have any type of control?

00:16:45.871 --> 00:16:47.940
- Yeah.
- It's on. Right?

00:16:47.940 --> 00:16:49.875
If I don't have any control,
like something like this,

00:16:49.875 --> 00:16:51.443
you gotta move on, you know?

00:16:51.443 --> 00:16:55.681
And so, in the game
of basketball there's always context.

00:16:55.981 --> 00:16:59.752
You know, there's five guys on the court,
there's only one ball, it's a 48 minute

00:16:59.752 --> 00:17:05.491
game and all the playing time
and all the roles get adjusted.

00:17:05.491 --> 00:17:09.294
So talk to Nico, talk to J. Kidd,
and, you know, we think we're gonna

00:17:09.294 --> 00:17:10.129
have a better team.

00:17:10.129 --> 00:17:12.698
And that's no disrespect to Jalen, right?
- Mmhmm.

00:17:12.698 --> 00:17:17.736
- Jalen had an amazing role, but adding
Christian Wood, you know, having Spencer,

00:17:18.137 --> 00:17:22.975
you know, further away from his injury
and more comfortable in their roles.

00:17:22.975 --> 00:17:27.146
I mean, for us to to pick up the,
you know, the guys that we did

00:17:27.146 --> 00:17:30.115
and go to the Western Conference
Finals, that says a lot.

00:17:30.115 --> 00:17:32.151
And so, and obviously JB was a big part.

00:17:32.151 --> 00:17:36.121
But I think, you know, JB's
primary role for us was scoring,

00:17:36.355 --> 00:17:38.991
and I think we more than compensated
and made up for that with Christian.

00:17:39.158 --> 00:17:39.491
- Yeah.

00:17:39.491 --> 00:17:42.795
So you feel like this Mavericks team
we'll see next season is a better team than the—

00:17:42.795 --> 00:17:43.562
- Yeah, without question.

00:17:43.562 --> 00:17:47.332
Because one of our real failings
last year was rebounding and shot-blocking

00:17:47.533 --> 00:17:51.503
and having—you know, and when we played
five out, we could play that with Max,

00:17:51.503 --> 00:17:54.339
meaning, you know, all five players out
beyond the three-point line.

00:17:55.040 --> 00:17:57.009
But we'll be even better at it with Christian.
- Yeah.

00:17:57.009 --> 00:17:57.810
- And with Maxi

00:17:57.810 --> 00:18:01.046
to come off the bench to support that,
I think we'll be a much better team,

00:18:01.046 --> 00:18:04.883
which will open up more space for Luka,
and the ball will move a little bit more.

00:18:04.917 --> 00:18:06.952
- Yeah. Well, for someone who,
you talk about,

00:18:06.952 --> 00:18:08.987
you know, the things you can control
versus things you don't,

00:18:08.987 --> 00:18:12.624
I would assume you don't really like to
not to be in control of things.
- Well, of course.

00:18:12.691 --> 00:18:14.426
- Yeah. Like, you wanna be able to control things.

00:18:14.426 --> 00:18:16.895
So obviously when you're sitting
watching the game,

00:18:16.895 --> 00:18:19.298
you can't do anything—
- Nope.
- Cuz they're just playing.
- Right.

00:18:19.665 --> 00:18:22.801
- So when you're watching,
like, what percentage of the game is fun?

00:18:22.801 --> 00:18:26.405
What percentage is stress? Like, what's that—
- 99.99 percent stress—
- OK.

00:18:26.572 --> 00:18:30.642
- Until we've won.
- Yeah.
- People don't realize that because—it's just like, people see me

00:18:30.642 --> 00:18:34.379
screaming and yelling,
and it's just like it's stress relief.

00:18:34.713 --> 00:18:35.280
- Mmhmm.
- You know?

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:37.983
And they'll see me after a big game
where we've won,

00:18:38.817 --> 00:18:42.087
and I'm just hoopin' and hollerin'
because that's letting all the stress out,

00:18:42.588 --> 00:18:44.423
you know?
And particularly in the playoffs,

00:18:44.423 --> 00:18:47.192
where there's so much at stake.
During the regular season,

00:18:47.192 --> 00:18:50.329
there's another game maybe. You know,
there's a lot of consequences to this game,

00:18:50.562 --> 00:18:53.765
and it's big,
but during the playoffs, it's all stress.

00:18:53.799 --> 00:18:56.435
- Yeah.
- 100 percent stress.
- Yeah, I would think so.

00:18:56.435 --> 00:19:00.105
And I think, you know,
you broke an 11-year drought—

00:19:00.506 --> 00:19:03.709
- Yep.
- You know, of not winning a playoff series.

00:19:03.709 --> 00:19:05.878
I'm sure that was a lot of stress.
- A lotta stress, yes.

00:19:05.878 --> 00:19:11.483
- Yeah.
- So when that shot didn't go in from
Bogdanovic from Utah, you have no idea.

00:19:11.650 --> 00:19:13.051
No idea.
- [Laughing] Yeah.

00:19:13.051 --> 00:19:17.823
- It was just like the skies parted,
and the sun was shining,

00:19:17.823 --> 00:19:22.261
and the entire organization and city were like, "[Exhaling]."
- "We did it!"

00:19:22.261 --> 00:19:22.928
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:19:22.928 --> 00:19:25.397
- But then, you know, and then,
you know, going to Phoenix

00:19:26.064 --> 00:19:27.900
and getting blown out
those first two games.

00:19:27.900 --> 00:19:32.738
And I remember being next to the bench,
and I wasn't really paying attention

00:19:32.738 --> 00:19:33.539
to the fans.

00:19:33.539 --> 00:19:34.173
And I went to Darrell,

00:19:34.173 --> 00:19:37.442
"What are they saying?" They're like, "They're
chanting 'Suns in four, Suns in four.''

00:19:37.809 --> 00:19:41.113
And I'm like, "Ohhh, OK, we'll see about that."
- Yeah.
- You know?

00:19:41.113 --> 00:19:45.951
And so, yeah, it's just, it's all stress, Taylor. All stress.
- No, I would assume so.

00:19:45.951 --> 00:19:52.324
So of those 11 years,
which year was the hardest?

00:19:52.324 --> 00:19:57.663
- I'd say the year we trade—
when we brought in Harrison

00:19:57.663 --> 00:20:01.466
and Andrew Bogut and—who
else did we have?

00:20:01.466 --> 00:20:03.068
We had CP25,

00:20:03.068 --> 00:20:07.372
we had Chandler Parsons and Monta,
I think, and I thought

00:20:07.372 --> 00:20:09.107
we were gonna have a good team,
and it just didn't turn out.

00:20:09.107 --> 00:20:11.143
And Deron Williams,
that's who we brought in.

00:20:11.143 --> 00:20:12.578
- Yeah.
- And it just didn't turn out that way.

00:20:12.578 --> 00:20:17.549
So when we started trading everybody,
that was, OK, it's time to rebuild.

00:20:17.849 --> 00:20:19.084
You know, we start going young.

00:20:19.084 --> 00:20:22.120
And so that was hard
cuz we had won 50 games

00:20:22.120 --> 00:20:24.590
I don't even know how many years,
and I'd never had a losing season.

00:20:24.590 --> 00:20:27.826
- Yeah.
- You know?
- Which is—like, that's so impressive, I hope you—

00:20:27.826 --> 00:20:30.562
- Yeah, well…well, that's Dirk, right?
- Yeah.

00:20:30.562 --> 00:20:33.365
- And so, that's the definition of a superstar.
- Yup.

00:20:33.365 --> 00:20:36.168
- Right? When you're always gonna win 50 games
and not have losing seasons.

00:20:36.168 --> 00:20:39.571
And so that was the toughest
because I really thought

00:20:39.571 --> 00:20:41.039
we were gonna move forward.
- Yeah.

00:20:41.039 --> 00:20:44.076
So I was talking to my producer, Rocky, and
she was telling me about a friend, a conversation

00:20:44.076 --> 00:20:47.079
she had with her friend, just about,
like, what this team means for you.

00:20:47.679 --> 00:20:50.215
So is this team an investment?

00:20:50.215 --> 00:20:51.750
Is it a passion?

00:20:51.750 --> 00:20:52.651
Is it a challenge?

00:20:52.651 --> 00:20:54.686
How do you think about the team?

00:20:54.853 --> 00:20:56.321
- I'm a "ball is life" guy,

00:20:56.321 --> 00:21:01.026
you know? Literally, like, my stress
release is going in my backyard

00:21:01.293 --> 00:21:04.763
and getting up shots and,
you know, working on my form,

00:21:05.030 --> 00:21:07.199
you know, or going to play pickup,
you know?
- Yeah.

00:21:07.199 --> 00:21:08.333
- You know, to tell you

00:21:08.333 --> 00:21:12.204
my feelings on basketball,
I got a hard workout in Saturday morning.

00:21:12.404 --> 00:21:14.106
And these guys, you know,

00:21:14.106 --> 00:21:18.277
all but maybe one was a lot, like, lot
younger than me. Like a third my age.

00:21:18.577 --> 00:21:20.379
And they're like, "We need one."

00:21:20.379 --> 00:21:23.115
And I'm like, so tired,
and I'm just so beat up.

00:21:23.181 --> 00:21:23.615
- Yeah.

00:21:23.682 --> 00:21:25.050
- But when you get, you know,

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:27.819
when you're walking by a court
and they say, "We need one"—

00:21:28.453 --> 00:21:29.888
- You're like—
- You gotta play, right?

00:21:29.888 --> 00:21:31.556
I don't care what it is.
- "That's me!

00:21:31.556 --> 00:21:32.691
Pick me."
- You could have crutches,

00:21:32.691 --> 00:21:34.826
and you gotta put 'em down and say—
- Yeah.
- "I'm gonna play."

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:39.831
And so, yeah, that's my attitude towards
basketball, and that's the connection.

00:21:39.831 --> 00:21:44.403
And then owning the team and
the financial side of it's secondary.

00:21:44.403 --> 00:21:45.504
- Yeah. OK.

00:21:45.504 --> 00:21:48.740
You've said that
business is the ultimate sport.

00:21:48.740 --> 00:21:50.575
- Yeah, it is.
- Explain what you mean by that.

00:21:50.575 --> 00:21:52.377
You know, I mean, basketball,

00:21:52.377 --> 00:21:54.446
you play 82 games plus the playoffs.

00:21:54.446 --> 00:21:55.981
You know who you're gonna play.

00:21:55.981 --> 00:21:58.016
You have time to prepare.
There's an offseason.

00:21:58.016 --> 00:22:00.152
You get some time off. In business,

00:22:00.152 --> 00:22:02.354
you don't even know who your competition may be.
- Yeah.

00:22:02.654 --> 00:22:06.625
- And you're competing with them 24
by seven by 365 by forever.

00:22:06.925 --> 00:22:09.061
You know, if I start a business,

00:22:09.061 --> 00:22:12.030
and you're in my industry,
I'm coming to kick your ass.

00:22:12.497 --> 00:22:16.635
- Yeah.
- And if you start a business, and you're in
my industry, I'm coming to kick your ass,

00:22:16.635 --> 00:22:16.935
right?

00:22:16.935 --> 00:22:19.204
And so that's why I keep

00:22:19.204 --> 00:22:22.908
on, you know, investing in companies,
starting companies, whatever it may be,

00:22:23.175 --> 00:22:26.211
because that competitive side of me,
that's where the juices flow.

00:22:26.511 --> 00:22:30.649
- Yeah.
- And it's just that ongoing, nonstop intellectual challenge

00:22:30.982 --> 00:22:34.820
to be great at whatever industry I'm in.
- Mmhmm. OK—
- Doesn't always work.

00:22:34.853 --> 00:22:36.188
- Yeah.
- But yeah.

00:22:36.188 --> 00:22:39.758
- But your goal is to be great.
- Yeah, not gonna go 82-0 in business either, so.

00:22:39.791 --> 00:22:42.194
- Yeah. OK, so if business is a sport—
- Yeah.

00:22:42.394 --> 00:22:44.262
- Tell me your NBA comparison.

00:22:44.262 --> 00:22:46.865
Who are you in the sport of business?

00:22:47.632 --> 00:22:49.434
- I'm Wilt. I'm Jordan.

00:22:49.434 --> 00:22:51.336
I'm LeBron. I'm Luka.
- [Laughing]

00:22:51.336 --> 00:22:56.441
- I'm Dirk. I'm Magic. I'm Bird, all rolled into one.
- You're all of it, yeah.
- Rolled into one.

00:22:56.575 --> 00:22:59.344
- All into one! You can't touch me!
- I love that.

00:23:00.212 --> 00:23:03.782
And this is, so this is my thing:
I think it's just, it's really interesting

00:23:03.782 --> 00:23:06.118
when somebody is good at business

00:23:06.351 --> 00:23:09.254
cuz I almost think business
is just, like, this umbrella term.

00:23:09.254 --> 00:23:10.155
Everybody thinks they know,

00:23:10.155 --> 00:23:13.291
like, what it means to, like,
be an entrepreneur or be a businessman.

00:23:13.291 --> 00:23:17.929
But it actually feels like a very specific
thing when you really think about it.

00:23:18.330 --> 00:23:19.097
- To be an entrepreneur, yeah.
- Yeah.

00:23:19.097 --> 00:23:21.066
- Yeah.
- Yeah.
- You—

00:23:21.066 --> 00:23:22.300
- How would you describe it?

00:23:22.300 --> 00:23:27.038
- You've got to be willing to be all
in, right?

00:23:27.038 --> 00:23:31.610
You know, some—in sports,
you can be athletically gifted, right?

00:23:31.610 --> 00:23:33.612
And that'll carry you a while.

00:23:33.612 --> 00:23:37.015
And then, you know,
if you wanna be one of the top 450, it's

00:23:37.015 --> 00:23:40.385
not only being athletically gifted, it's,
you know, what's your skill set

00:23:40.385 --> 00:23:42.220
and how do you work on it
and what's your basketball

00:23:42.220 --> 00:23:47.092
IQ? And business is very similar,
except that it's ever-changing.
- Mmhmm.

00:23:47.592 --> 00:23:50.262
You know, we talk about,
you know, three-point

00:23:50.262 --> 00:23:54.032
play in analytics changed basketball
some and, you know, less than the big man,

00:23:54.032 --> 00:23:56.635
whatever. And business is changing every day.
- Yeah.

00:23:56.701 --> 00:23:59.438
- You know, there's some new technology,
there's some new approach,

00:23:59.438 --> 00:24:02.707
there's some new rule,
there's some new tax law, whatever it may be.

00:24:02.707 --> 00:24:04.776
So the rules are always changing.

00:24:04.776 --> 00:24:06.344
And so you've gotta be curious.

00:24:06.344 --> 00:24:08.346
You've gotta be agile.
You gotta be able to sell.

00:24:08.346 --> 00:24:09.848
You gotta be able to communicate.

00:24:09.848 --> 00:24:11.316
You gotta be empathetic.

00:24:11.316 --> 00:24:13.552
You've gotta be considerate.
You gotta be nice.

00:24:13.552 --> 00:24:14.820
There's all these different skills

00:24:14.820 --> 00:24:17.022
that come together
to be good as an entrepreneur.

00:24:17.022 --> 00:24:20.325
- Yeah. Well, speaking of that, first off,
as you can see, I like lists.

00:24:20.792 --> 00:24:24.563
What three NBA players
would be the best salesmen? And why?

00:24:26.264 --> 00:24:27.065
- Um…well, I gotta put Luka.

00:24:27.065 --> 00:24:29.334
Luka could sell anything.

00:24:29.801 --> 00:24:31.369
That guy is so street smart.

00:24:31.369 --> 00:24:32.671
You have no idea.

00:24:32.671 --> 00:24:34.973
- Really?
- Oh, my God.

00:24:34.973 --> 00:24:36.341
He'll FaceTime me with some—

00:24:36.341 --> 00:24:39.277
yeah, never even—
- No, no!
- No, no, no!
- You can't start and stop!

00:24:39.277 --> 00:24:44.249
"He'll FaceTime me and—"
- Like, when he's out having fun with something, right?

00:24:44.249 --> 00:24:47.786
- Yeah.
- And so with all his boys and whoever out
hanging out with them,

00:24:48.954 --> 00:24:51.656
and you'll see a lotta stuff—
- That is not what you were gonna say at first.

00:24:51.656 --> 00:24:55.861
- No, but—
- [Laughing]
- You'll see, you know—
- I'm like, "What?"
- Luka's got an amazing team around him,

00:24:55.861 --> 00:24:58.263
and so you'll see a lotta really,
really cool tech stuff,

00:24:58.630 --> 00:25:01.733
you know, that he helped engineer,
literally,

00:25:01.733 --> 00:25:02.934
that is gonna be really cool, so.
- Yeah.

00:25:02.934 --> 00:25:05.303
- I say Luka is a great salesperson.

00:25:05.670 --> 00:25:09.541
Jordan, obviously, with Jordans,
you know? Michael is great

00:25:09.541 --> 00:25:14.179
at what he does there.
- Mmhmm.
- And who out of left field would be a great

00:25:14.179 --> 00:25:15.480
sales—who have I dealt with?

00:25:16.815 --> 00:25:17.949
We've had—

00:25:18.183 --> 00:25:21.152
Theo Pinson—
- Really?
- Probably is, yeah.
- He has a very, like,

00:25:21.152 --> 00:25:26.191
vivacious personality.
- Yeah, right. Just like, you know,
and it's funny because he did

00:25:26.191 --> 00:25:29.294
this sales thing. I'm like, "Theo,
come on. You're a good salesperson.

00:25:29.294 --> 00:25:30.795
Let me give you some hints."

00:25:30.795 --> 00:25:31.129
- Yeah.

00:25:31.129 --> 00:25:34.332
- You know, and so I think, I think he's
got that potential to be really good.

00:25:34.366 --> 00:25:35.133
- Yeah. OK. So speaking of—

00:25:35.133 --> 00:25:37.369
- And Spencer, too. Spencer Dinwiddie is really—

00:25:37.502 --> 00:25:37.903
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:25:37.903 --> 00:25:39.538
He's a hustler.
- And he already kinda does that.
- All the time.

00:25:39.538 --> 00:25:44.476
- Like, he's a big—he's like finance-y and
doing all these, like, innovative things.
- He’s smart.

00:25:44.476 --> 00:25:47.846
- He’s very, very smart.
- He's learned how to pull all the pieces together

00:25:47.846 --> 00:25:51.983
to leverage his brand to, you know,
enhance his brand. And he digs in.

00:25:51.983 --> 00:25:54.586
He doesn't jump from one thing
to a next thing, which is good.
- Yeah.

00:25:54.719 --> 00:25:55.186
OK.

00:25:55.186 --> 00:25:57.389
So on the subject of business,

00:25:57.389 --> 00:26:00.392
what does money start meaning to you
when you've made so much?

00:26:01.293 --> 00:26:03.295
- It really depends
where you are in your life, right?

00:26:03.295 --> 00:26:08.133
Like, when you're 21, 31,
even 41, it's about potential, right?

00:26:08.133 --> 00:26:12.037
As you get older,
it's more about family and impact

00:26:12.470 --> 00:26:15.907
because, you know, you start, like—
when I sold my first company, it was like,

00:26:16.041 --> 00:26:20.312
Oh, my God, I just want to retire and party
with as many people as possible.

00:26:20.312 --> 00:26:21.313
Bought a lifetime pass

00:26:21.313 --> 00:26:22.647
in American Airlines

00:26:22.647 --> 00:26:25.517
and party like a rock star
and put a lot of rock stars to shame.

00:26:25.817 --> 00:26:26.318
And, you know,

00:26:27.519 --> 00:26:31.356
and so, you know,
they couldn’t hang with me.

00:26:31.356 --> 00:26:35.060
And then bought the Mavs,
and that opened up some other doors.

00:26:35.293 --> 00:26:36.194
But then, you know,

00:26:36.194 --> 00:26:39.631
you look at other sports teams, is
this what I want to do or whatever else.

00:26:40.398 --> 00:26:44.169
But I’ve never been about, like, stuff.
Let me have more houses,

00:26:44.169 --> 00:26:46.838
more cars, more money.
Let me be higher on the Forbes 400.

00:26:47.072 --> 00:26:49.341
None of that stuff at all
really was important to me.

00:26:49.708 --> 00:26:51.910
But as you get older,
you know, and have a family,

00:26:51.910 --> 00:26:54.212
then it's like, OK, kids,
and where are they gonna be.

00:26:54.212 --> 00:26:55.380
And, you know, my next

00:26:55.380 --> 00:26:59.050
dollar is not going to change my life,
but it can change my family's life,

00:26:59.317 --> 00:27:03.722
their family's life, their kids’ life
and maybe some other people as well.

00:27:03.722 --> 00:27:07.459
And that, at least with me,
that started taking on more importance:

00:27:07.459 --> 00:27:08.627
What kind of impact could I have?

00:27:08.627 --> 00:27:09.961
- Yeah, absolutely. OK.

00:27:09.961 --> 00:27:12.831
I do want to pause and go back to Party
Mark real quick.
- Sure.

00:27:12.931 --> 00:27:17.936
- Because in a lot of interviews I've seen,
I've seen you talk about Party Mark.

00:27:18.303 --> 00:27:22.741
So tell me the best party story
that you have.

00:27:22.741 --> 00:27:24.776
I mean, you said you're
putting rock stars to shame.

00:27:24.776 --> 00:27:29.481
I mean, what, what does that mean? Who?

00:27:29.481 --> 00:27:32.317
- I mean, there's more
than I could ever possibly tell.
- Tell me the best one.

00:27:33.084 --> 00:27:34.052
- So let's put it this way.

00:27:34.052 --> 00:27:36.121
If we went out
and I revved it up,

00:27:36.121 --> 00:27:40.125
you couldn’t hang.
- Really? You think so?
- No doubt. There is no doubt.
- I don't know.

00:27:40.125 --> 00:27:43.094
I've had some fun in my day.
- Yeah.

00:27:43.828 --> 00:27:44.696
Trust me.

00:27:45.730 --> 00:27:46.464
Yeah.

00:27:46.665 --> 00:27:48.633
- Who have you put to shame?

00:27:48.633 --> 00:27:52.937
- There's, there's quite a few NBA players
over the years that we've gone toe-to-toe,

00:27:53.872 --> 00:27:54.939
and yeah.

00:27:54.939 --> 00:27:57.308
I'm not going to name-drop who they are.

00:27:57.509 --> 00:27:59.544
- It's only fun when you tell the story.

00:27:59.544 --> 00:28:01.012
- I know! But, you know—

00:28:01.012 --> 00:28:04.582
- Listen, if you could tell a story
about when Bill Gates took your girl

00:28:04.582 --> 00:28:07.185
- Yeah, I know.
- You can tell me one person you partied with.

00:28:07.385 --> 00:28:09.521
- Oh, a lot. There's not just one person.

00:28:09.521 --> 00:28:12.924
It's just like—one time
we were out for Juwan

00:28:12.924 --> 00:28:13.958
Howard’s—

00:28:13.958 --> 00:28:15.026
this was when,

00:28:15.026 --> 00:28:16.428
when I failed miserably.

00:28:16.428 --> 00:28:18.363
I'll give you one when I failed, OK?

00:28:18.363 --> 00:28:23.068
So we were out for Juwan Howard's
birthday party in New York City.

00:28:23.668 --> 00:28:24.903
It was right before the All-Star break.

00:28:24.903 --> 00:28:29.240
We had just played the Knicks, I think,
and we had an extra few days, right?

00:28:29.240 --> 00:28:32.310
So we were going to go out. And it was me,

00:28:32.310 --> 00:28:36.081
Fin [Michael Finley], Dirk, Nash and I forget

00:28:36.081 --> 00:28:39.884
who else. [Al] Whitley maybe? Yeah,
Whits I think and some other folks.

00:28:40.452 --> 00:28:42.153
And we went to,

00:28:43.221 --> 00:28:45.223
what was it called, Lotus or Lotus

00:28:45.490 --> 00:28:49.794
something, and Steve
Nash ended up meeting his then-wife.

00:28:49.794 --> 00:28:50.395
That's how crazy the night was.

00:28:50.395 --> 00:28:53.732
- Oh, wow.
- But there was a,

00:28:53.732 --> 00:28:57.702
there was a buddy of Al’s
that used to wear this…

00:28:57.936 --> 00:28:59.170
I can't say that.

00:28:59.170 --> 00:29:04.876
But we go in there and we, I start—

00:29:05.043 --> 00:29:07.412
we, and then me in particular, start

00:29:07.979 --> 00:29:10.882
buying I think it was Wild Turkey by the bottle.

00:29:10.949 --> 00:29:11.649
- Oh, wow.

00:29:11.649 --> 00:29:15.420
- And drinking it out of the bottle.

00:29:15.420 --> 00:29:16.988
It was ugly.

00:29:16.988 --> 00:29:18.056
It was ugly.

00:29:18.056 --> 00:29:21.092
So let's just say
that it wasn't long after that, my head

00:29:21.092 --> 00:29:25.029
was hanging out the
the window of the limo, getting sick,

00:29:25.363 --> 00:29:30.001
and all these guys were laughing at me,
and thank God it was pre-iPhone.

00:29:30.435 --> 00:29:31.102
Otherwise

00:29:32.103 --> 00:29:33.271
I could never live it down.

00:29:33.271 --> 00:29:35.974
I still can't live it down
with these guys.

00:29:35.974 --> 00:29:38.276
- So who's like the comfort in the group
at that moment?

00:29:38.276 --> 00:29:43.648
Like, was Dirk like, “It's OK, Mark.”
- Hell no. Are you kidding me?

00:29:44.048 --> 00:29:45.450
Are you kidding me?

00:29:45.450 --> 00:29:50.355
They're like, oh, they were
throwing me under the bus for years.

00:29:50.855 --> 00:29:52.257
For years.

00:29:52.257 --> 00:29:54.692
But, you know, that's the exception
that proves the rule.
- Yeah, no.

00:29:54.759 --> 00:29:55.426
Totally.

00:29:55.426 --> 00:29:56.961
Because, you know, I was like,
you got to have the friend

00:29:56.961 --> 00:29:59.063
that, like, holds your hair back
if you got to throw up.

00:29:59.330 --> 00:30:01.466
- Oh, my God.
- You gotta have that one.

00:30:01.466 --> 00:30:02.901
But I think, yeah, guys are different.

00:30:02.901 --> 00:30:05.069
They get on each other,
like, “You can't hang.”

00:30:05.403 --> 00:30:07.572
It’s definitely a different thing.
- Yeah.

00:30:07.572 --> 00:30:10.942
The good news is I got worse
stories on Dirk and all those guys, so.

00:30:10.975 --> 00:30:12.677
- Yeah, so you have that as blackmail.

00:30:12.677 --> 00:30:16.314
If you did iPhone it,
don't ever show cuz I got you, Dirk.

00:30:16.381 --> 00:30:18.116
- Absolutely.
- Dirk is so fun.

00:30:18.116 --> 00:30:20.318
Like, I genuinely really like Dirk.
- He’s a good dude.

00:30:20.318 --> 00:30:22.353
- He's just, like, a nice guy.

00:30:22.587 --> 00:30:24.122
- I love him to death. He's a great guy.

00:30:24.122 --> 00:30:25.723
- Yeah.
- Great guy. A heart of gold.

00:30:27.992 --> 00:30:29.761
Just says the dumbest, funnest—

00:30:29.761 --> 00:30:33.798
You know, he's just a good dude.
You can’t say enough great things.

00:30:34.032 --> 00:30:34.699
- So it's super

00:30:34.699 --> 00:30:38.469
clear to me that, like, the relationships
that you have are important to you.

00:30:38.469 --> 00:30:41.539
And obviously relationships are important
in any business.

00:30:41.539 --> 00:30:45.009
It's one of the things that makes business
men and women very successful.

00:30:45.410 --> 00:30:48.980
But I feel like relationships in
the NBA can be hard

00:30:48.980 --> 00:30:50.882
because it is such a business

00:30:50.882 --> 00:30:53.451
and everybody has, like, one thing
that they're trying to accomplish.

00:30:54.018 --> 00:30:58.022
Is there anyone that has been
in the league that you would like to mend

00:30:58.022 --> 00:31:04.762
a relationship with?

00:31:04.762 --> 00:31:08.733
- Golly, that's a great question.

00:31:08.733 --> 00:31:10.568
I mean, it was rocky with Steve Nash

00:31:10.568 --> 00:31:14.439
for a while after he left,
and I'd like to think we'd mended that.

00:31:15.940 --> 00:31:16.841
We hung out a couple of

00:31:16.841 --> 00:31:25.383
times and talked,
and I think we're on the same page.

00:31:25.383 --> 00:31:29.754
You know, I think we could have parted
ways with Monta Ellis a little better way.

00:31:30.054 --> 00:31:32.123
You know, it was—

00:31:33.625 --> 00:31:35.994
things just changed
and that created issues.

00:31:35.994 --> 00:31:38.863
And I think I didn't handle it
as well as I could’ve.

00:31:39.998 --> 00:31:41.532
Like, you're going to make mistakes.
- Yeah.

00:31:41.532 --> 00:31:45.703
- And the other hard part is
generations are different.

00:31:46.104 --> 00:31:48.640
You know, when I first came into the league,
we were still on the tail

00:31:48.640 --> 00:31:53.044
end of the macho generation and the league
was telling AI how to dress

00:31:53.044 --> 00:31:55.079
and so having a dress code and all that.

00:31:56.281 --> 00:31:59.450
And then in the 2010s, you know,

00:31:59.550 --> 00:32:05.223
the older millennials, I guess, now were,
you know, approached basketball different.

00:32:05.223 --> 00:32:07.191
And you just started to see

00:32:07.191 --> 00:32:10.795
the advent of social media and Facebook
and the like.

00:32:10.795 --> 00:32:14.532
And, you know, but ESPN and TV
were still really, really big.

00:32:14.899 --> 00:32:17.969
And then,
you know, kids coming in Luka’s generation—

00:32:17.969 --> 00:32:20.471
Zion, Ja—it's all social media.

00:32:20.571 --> 00:32:21.039
- Yeah.

00:32:21.039 --> 00:32:24.375
- They grew up completely driven,
not necessarily driven,

00:32:25.143 --> 00:32:28.212
excuse me,
but they grew up, you know, organically

00:32:28.212 --> 00:32:32.450
with social media. It was, you know,
they were stars when they were teenagers,

00:32:32.550 --> 00:32:33.818
you know,

00:32:33.818 --> 00:32:37.121
teenagers coming into the NBA. You know,
they could have been stars when they were

00:32:37.121 --> 00:32:38.790
11, 12, 13 years old.

00:32:38.790 --> 00:32:43.328
And so, you know, they're very much
more cognizant of their brand,

00:32:44.062 --> 00:32:45.063
and it's going to get even worse

00:32:45.063 --> 00:32:46.664
now with NIL. You're going to have kids

00:32:46.664 --> 00:32:48.900
making so much money
in high school and college.

00:32:48.900 --> 00:32:51.936
Pros may not be increase in pay,

00:32:52.203 --> 00:32:56.808
you know? But the point being that
generations in this game

00:32:56.808 --> 00:33:02.080
change, and teams have to adapt,
and relationships have to adapt.

00:33:02.447 --> 00:33:06.751
And, you know, when I was as young
as some of the players, it was easy.

00:33:06.884 --> 00:33:09.721
- Yeah.
- Now it’s harder, you know, because they look at me

00:33:09.721 --> 00:33:12.323
and like, “OK that's just the owner dude.”
- Right. Yeah.

00:33:12.490 --> 00:33:14.192
I mean, I could tell
even when I asked and you were,

00:33:14.192 --> 00:33:16.794
you were thinking about it,
and you mentioned Steve and Monta.

00:33:16.794 --> 00:33:19.564
Are those things that you think about now
still?

00:33:19.964 --> 00:33:22.066
- Not as much.
I mean, because he gets consumed

00:33:22.066 --> 00:33:24.469
with what's going on and what's in
front of you.
- Yeah.

00:33:26.070 --> 00:33:27.972
What would you have done differently?

00:33:27.972 --> 00:33:30.008
With Steve, I would have kept him.

00:33:30.041 --> 00:33:31.743
With Monta,

00:33:31.743 --> 00:33:33.578
I just remember having a meeting with him,
and I just,

00:33:33.578 --> 00:33:35.813
I just don't think I handled it
as well as I could, because Monta,

00:33:36.180 --> 00:33:38.583
you know, I think he's a good guy
with a great heart.

00:33:38.583 --> 00:33:40.318
- Yeah, no. Absolutely.

00:33:40.318 --> 00:33:42.754
I mean, I think that just in general, like,

00:33:42.754 --> 00:33:46.391
owners can be put in these, like,
very sticky situations

00:33:46.391 --> 00:33:47.492
cuz you're having to manage

00:33:47.492 --> 00:33:51.095
so many different things at all times
to the best of your ability.

00:33:51.262 --> 00:33:54.132
And like, just, you know, the,
the example that, that I'll use

00:33:54.132 --> 00:33:55.967
is everything
that's happening in Brooklyn, right?

00:33:55.967 --> 00:33:57.835
You, like, have to do the right thing

00:33:57.835 --> 00:34:00.638
because one wrong move,
it might mess everything up.

00:34:00.938 --> 00:34:04.442
- The hard part is
I think that people don't realize is

00:34:04.742 --> 00:34:08.312
you don't buy a team
to take on a job, right?

00:34:08.312 --> 00:34:10.181
I didn’t buy the Mavs
because I wanted a job.

00:34:10.181 --> 00:34:12.750
- Yeah.
- I bought the Mavs
because I was done having jobs.

00:34:12.750 --> 00:34:14.752
- Yeah. That’s true. Yeah.
- You know?

00:34:14.752 --> 00:34:16.821
I bought the Mavs because I love basketball

00:34:17.055 --> 00:34:18.423
and, you know, I love the Mavericks.

00:34:18.423 --> 00:34:19.490
I was a season ticket holder,

00:34:19.490 --> 00:34:22.260
and I wanted to see them do better,
and I wanted to enjoy it.

00:34:22.560 --> 00:34:25.830
And you wanna hire people to
do the things, and you hope it works out.

00:34:26.264 --> 00:34:28.699
The hard part is when you make mistakes
where it doesn't work out.

00:34:28.699 --> 00:34:29.434
- Yeah.
- Right?

00:34:29.434 --> 00:34:34.372
Because, you know, your name's on the check.
- Mmhmm.
- And it's your ultimate responsibility.

00:34:34.872 --> 00:34:39.277
And, you know, whether it's Brooklyn
or whoever is put in those circumstances,

00:34:39.944 --> 00:34:44.282
you know, the owner is not involved
because they wanna be involved.

00:34:44.282 --> 00:34:47.752
- Right.
- They're involved because it's fallen on them
to have to be involved.
- Yeah.

00:34:47.852 --> 00:34:50.121
Cuz that's the thing
like with Brooklyn, right?

00:34:50.121 --> 00:34:53.291
Like how do you navigate being the story?

00:34:53.825 --> 00:34:56.360
Like, when what everyone is talking about

00:34:56.360 --> 00:34:59.564
the team, it's you, like,
the owner is the story.

00:34:59.597 --> 00:35:00.998
Does that make sense?
- No, it makes total sense.

00:35:00.998 --> 00:35:03.201
And it really depends on who the owner is.

00:35:03.334 --> 00:35:04.102
- Yeah
- You know,

00:35:05.403 --> 00:35:07.805
I'm used to being famous, right?

00:35:08.005 --> 00:35:09.607
But being famous can be hard.
- Mmhmm.

00:35:09.607 --> 00:35:11.742
- And being famous is even harder
when you don't wanna be.

00:35:11.776 --> 00:35:14.645
- Yeah.
- You know, when you don't
wanna be the center of attention,

00:35:14.645 --> 00:35:17.415
and you just wanna enjoy your life,
enjoy your team and,

00:35:17.648 --> 00:35:18.850
you know, enjoy the game.

00:35:18.850 --> 00:35:21.119
And, you know, I don't know
Joe [Tsai] that well.

00:35:21.385 --> 00:35:22.587
I'm gettin' to know him better.

00:35:22.587 --> 00:35:23.588
I don't know why, but

00:35:23.588 --> 00:35:26.357
I never asked him why he bought the team
or what's the importance of it to him.

00:35:26.657 --> 00:35:28.626
- Yeah.
- It may be
because his kids love basketball.

00:35:28.626 --> 00:35:32.430
Maybe it's because, you know,
he wants a legacy business to turn over

00:35:32.430 --> 00:35:35.233
to, you know, a son or daughter
who may love basketball.

00:35:35.433 --> 00:35:37.702
You don't really know the reasons why.

00:35:38.002 --> 00:35:40.404
And so when you're in that position,
and all of a sudden,

00:35:41.205 --> 00:35:44.742
you know, it's
social media is just on fire, you know,

00:35:44.976 --> 00:35:49.413
and you can't really go
and look because it's just brutal.

00:35:49.547 --> 00:35:50.248
- Yeah.
- Right?

00:35:50.248 --> 00:35:53.985
It's really, you know, there's
no real easy way to deal with it.

00:35:54.418 --> 00:35:59.657
- Mmhmm.
- You know, and, look, you also have to
give yourself a reality check.

00:35:59.857 --> 00:36:01.292
It's first-world problems.

00:36:01.292 --> 00:36:02.093
- Totally.
- Right?

00:36:02.193 --> 00:36:05.530
- Oh, my team that I own
is going through some issues.

00:36:05.763 --> 00:36:09.400
- I mean, I've been broke, and having people
yell at me because the Mavs are doing so

00:36:09.400 --> 00:36:13.171
well is a whole lot
better than being broke, you know?
- Yeah.

00:36:13.571 --> 00:36:16.774
- You know, eatin' mustard and ketchup
sandwiches was—
- Was not it.

00:36:16.774 --> 00:36:21.779
- Yeah I'll take, you know,
"Sell the team you, S—, F—," whatever.

00:36:21.779 --> 00:36:24.782
You know, I can live with that,
but it takes some time to get used to.

00:36:24.949 --> 00:36:25.950
- Yeah, for sure.

00:36:25.950 --> 00:36:29.854
And I just think so
much of it is, like, just navigating things

00:36:29.854 --> 00:36:31.756
on all different levels.

00:36:31.756 --> 00:36:35.293
And so you've mentioned, like, social media a bunch.
What is your relationship

00:36:35.426 --> 00:36:36.260
with social media?

00:36:36.260 --> 00:36:37.028
- Yeah, it's fun

00:36:37.028 --> 00:36:40.231
cuz I've got a lot of different
personalities for social media, right?

00:36:40.231 --> 00:36:43.501
There's what I do with Cost Plus Drugs, there's
what I do for entrepreneurs, there's what I do

00:36:43.501 --> 00:36:46.204
with Shark Tank and what I do with the Mavs.
- Yeah.

00:36:46.237 --> 00:36:49.840
- And there's different levels of response
in each of those,

00:36:50.141 --> 00:36:53.010
you know. Shark Tank's all friendly,
you know, or "Why did you do this deal?"

00:36:53.010 --> 00:36:54.545
"You suck," whatever.

00:36:54.545 --> 00:36:56.981
Or, you know, "I didn't like the product."

00:36:56.981 --> 00:36:59.250
"Why did you invest," whatever.

00:36:59.250 --> 00:37:00.251
Um, that stuff's easy.

00:37:01.419 --> 00:37:02.286
Um, technology

00:37:02.286 --> 00:37:05.423
and entrepreneurship is easy,
even though people may disagree with you.

00:37:05.823 --> 00:37:11.462
But NBA Twitter can be brutal.
- Yeah, it can.
- Brutal.

00:37:11.462 --> 00:37:13.998
And you just gotta realize that,
you know,

00:37:14.498 --> 00:37:16.901
you know, fan is short for fanatic,
you know?
- Mmhmm.

00:37:16.901 --> 00:37:18.469
- And that's what I remind myself.

00:37:18.469 --> 00:37:23.474
The people who are most belligerent
or mean or evil or whatever—
- Yeah.

00:37:24.075 --> 00:37:27.245
- You wanna think that they're the ones
who love the Mavs or their team the most.
- Mmhmm.

00:37:27.245 --> 00:37:30.915
- And so I always, you know,
try to put that in context, but, you know,

00:37:31.115 --> 00:37:34.652
and it's the worst during the summer,
because everybody wants to win the summer.

00:37:34.719 --> 00:37:36.921
- Yeah.
- It's not about winning
championships, right?

00:37:36.988 --> 00:37:38.122
It's about winning the summer.

00:37:38.122 --> 00:37:39.624
"Well, so-and-so did this,"

00:37:39.624 --> 00:37:42.727
And "This guy hasn't signed a deal yet,"
or "This guy hasn't done that."
- Yeah.

00:37:42.727 --> 00:37:44.228
- And, "So why aren't you doing something?"

00:37:44.228 --> 00:37:45.930
"You piece of da-da-da-da-da."

00:37:47.131 --> 00:37:49.934
You know, like, you know, it's funny

00:37:49.934 --> 00:37:53.738
because there was a guy, and I happened
to be looking at my timeline, right?

00:37:53.738 --> 00:37:55.740
Cuz I was looking at Cost Plus Drug stuff.

00:37:55.740 --> 00:37:58.175
And in my timeline, his

00:37:58.743 --> 00:38:02.213
his handle was, "F Mark Cuban, sell the Mavs."

00:38:03.748 --> 00:38:05.650
And there's a lot of them,
it wasn't like there was just one.

00:38:05.650 --> 00:38:09.320
- Yeah.
- But this guy was really coming hard.
So he had his DMs open,

00:38:09.520 --> 00:38:11.822
and so I just sent him a DM saying,

00:38:11.822 --> 00:38:15.660
"I love you, too. Have a nice day,"
just because, you know, it's just like—

00:38:15.660 --> 00:38:17.828
- Yeah. Did he respond?
- No, no.

00:38:18.496 --> 00:38:20.131
- Is that still his name?

00:38:20.131 --> 00:38:22.333
- I don't know. Yeah,
it still's his name.
- Oh, it is.
- Yeah, still his name.

00:38:22.333 --> 00:38:23.634
But it happens all the time, like—

00:38:23.634 --> 00:38:26.304
- Yeah.
- Like, when I was—
I said something last year about how,

00:38:27.104 --> 00:38:30.708
you know, insane Mavs Twitter can be,
and then, you know,

00:38:30.708 --> 00:38:33.711
I went and offered a bunch of those people tickets.
- Mmhmm.
- Because, you know,

00:38:33.711 --> 00:38:36.781
what happens is people
use it as a platform to blow off steam,

00:38:36.781 --> 00:38:39.583
and it's a way for them to create,
you know, a community.

00:38:39.583 --> 00:38:43.788
There's, like—there's a community
for every team of the fans

00:38:43.788 --> 00:38:46.190
that hate everything
the front office does.
- Yes.
- Right?

00:38:46.190 --> 00:38:47.692
They love their best players.

00:38:47.692 --> 00:38:49.293
They hate their worst players.

00:38:49.293 --> 00:38:51.362
And—but it's a community.

00:38:51.362 --> 00:38:55.599
And when you actually talk to them,
they're not really mean and nasty.

00:38:55.900 --> 00:38:57.201
- Mmhmm.
- It's just Twitter.
- Right.

00:38:57.201 --> 00:39:00.004
- You know—
- I always—like, the internet's not a real place.

00:39:00.004 --> 00:39:02.373
- No, Twitter's not real.
- Yeah. Yeah.
- Not real life.
- It's not a real place.

00:39:02.873 --> 00:39:05.109
- So I try to give 'em a little love.
- Yeah.

00:39:05.142 --> 00:39:07.044
- And then they back down,
and then they—

00:39:07.044 --> 00:39:08.479
So, there's this one

00:39:08.612 --> 00:39:12.783
podcast/website, and I'm like, "Well,
just have me on," and have me on,

00:39:12.783 --> 00:39:15.886
ask me nothing but benign,
easy, simple questions, right?

00:39:16.487 --> 00:39:18.889
At the end of it, I'm like, "You
sure there's nothing else you wanna add?"

00:39:18.889 --> 00:39:19.390
- Yeah.

00:39:19.390 --> 00:39:20.791
- And then he gets back on Twitter,

00:39:20.791 --> 00:39:22.493
just crushes me every day.

00:39:22.493 --> 00:39:25.563
I'm like, dude—
- You had your opportunity.
- You had your chance.

00:39:25.563 --> 00:39:27.098
- Yeah, that is so funny.

00:39:27.098 --> 00:39:30.067
And also, too, with the internet,
I always trusted people. I'm like,

00:39:30.868 --> 00:39:35.639
all you have to do is get to the next day
cuz no one cares.

00:39:35.639 --> 00:39:36.841
- No one cares. That's the thing.

00:39:36.841 --> 00:39:38.909
The news cycles are so short.
- Yeah.

00:39:39.009 --> 00:39:40.711
- And people don't, you know, do their homework.

00:39:40.711 --> 00:39:42.246
They don't really care care.
- Right.

00:39:42.246 --> 00:39:45.649
- It's not like people dig in to find out
all the details and everything.

00:39:46.016 --> 00:39:47.651
They just wanna talk crap, right? And

00:39:48.853 --> 00:39:49.854
more power to 'em.
- It's, like, whatever.

00:39:49.854 --> 00:39:50.721
Yeah, go ahead.

00:39:50.721 --> 00:39:54.925
- Better you talking about the NBA
than talking about whoever?

00:39:55.126 --> 00:39:55.826
- Yeah. OK.

00:39:55.826 --> 00:39:59.263
So you tweeted about the play-in
tournament, speaking of Twitter.
- Uh huh.

00:39:59.263 --> 00:40:01.499
- You talked about the injuries.
- Uh huh.

00:40:01.499 --> 00:40:03.701
- First, I wanna ask how you
would restructure the plan,

00:40:03.868 --> 00:40:09.507
but also I wanna know what changed
because you did push for the plan.
- No,

00:40:09.507 --> 00:40:11.142
So, first of all, you know,

00:40:11.142 --> 00:40:15.713
leading into restarting the league
was one set of circumstances.

00:40:16.046 --> 00:40:17.681
So the plan was a good idea.

00:40:17.681 --> 00:40:22.720
And then as we got into it,
it wasn't so much that the play-in—

00:40:22.720 --> 00:40:29.460
So then we were playing in a season
that was shortened and was off schedule, right?

00:40:29.560 --> 00:40:30.961
- Mmhmm.
- It wasn't a normal schedule.

00:40:30.961 --> 00:40:33.531
So the plan had a different set
of circumstances as well.

00:40:33.798 --> 00:40:39.270
Then we get to this year,
and it's like that ninth and 10th team

00:40:39.570 --> 00:40:43.140
in both conferences
had some really bad teams, right?

00:40:43.240 --> 00:40:46.243
- Mmhmm.
- And you had this unexpected
set of circumstance, unintended

00:40:46.243 --> 00:40:49.046
consequences, where you had teams

00:40:49.880 --> 00:40:52.983
not only trying to get the best draft
pick, but trying to avoid the plan.
- Yeah.

00:40:53.551 --> 00:40:57.721
- And you created these circumstances
where you had to make a choice

00:40:57.721 --> 00:41:00.958
between having a top-six, seven
or eight pick,

00:41:01.225 --> 00:41:03.561
you know, maybe ninth pick
or making the play-in.

00:41:04.195 --> 00:41:06.330
And if you don't have a decent team,

00:41:06.764 --> 00:41:10.768
and you're a six, seven, eight,
nine pick is gonna really help you—
- Mmhmm.

00:41:11.702 --> 00:41:14.438
- You might sandbag a play-in game.
- Mmhmm.

00:41:14.672 --> 00:41:16.140
- So that was my attitude there.

00:41:16.140 --> 00:41:18.275
And so what I proposed,
and they turned it down,

00:41:18.576 --> 00:41:21.812
was that we go back to what we did
in the bubble where you had to be

00:41:21.812 --> 00:41:24.014
I think it was in four games,
within four games—
- Yeah.

00:41:24.148 --> 00:41:26.484
- In order to make the play-in,
which I thought is great.

00:41:26.851 --> 00:41:27.084
- Mmhmm.
- Right.

00:41:27.084 --> 00:41:29.520
But being ten or 11 games back,
and look what happened to the Clippers.

00:41:29.520 --> 00:41:32.456
They lose Paul George to COVID for one game.
- Mmhmm.

00:41:33.390 --> 00:41:36.660
And it, yeah.
- You're out.
- So that's how you would restructure,

00:41:36.660 --> 00:41:38.095
go back to how they did the bubble.

00:41:38.095 --> 00:41:40.397
- Yeah, that's exactly what I suggested.
- It's not that you're like, "No plan."

00:41:40.664 --> 00:41:42.566
- No.
- You're just like, there's a different way.

00:41:42.566 --> 00:41:44.168
- Yeah, you have—context always matters.

00:41:44.168 --> 00:41:45.069
- Yeah.
- And so now

00:41:45.069 --> 00:41:48.672
we're back to a regular live season
for the most part, 82 games.
- Mmhmm.

00:41:49.173 --> 00:41:53.310
- And so, you know, I was a proponent
of making it within four games,

00:41:53.310 --> 00:41:54.044
but they shot me down.

00:41:54.044 --> 00:41:56.881
- Yeah, well, the thing is,
and I know this isn't, like, your argument,

00:41:56.881 --> 00:42:00.251
but what somebody would just say is
like, "Well, then don't be in the bottom."

00:42:00.751 --> 00:42:04.021
- Well—
- You know.
- Yeah, you know,
I wish you can control injuries.

00:42:04.021 --> 00:42:06.790
- Yeah.
- I wish I could control all the other
teams and trades and all that.

00:42:06.790 --> 00:42:07.424
But you can't.

00:42:07.424 --> 00:42:07.725
- OK.

00:42:07.725 --> 00:42:09.960
Well, I mean, so
as we're kind of sticking with Twitter

00:42:09.960 --> 00:42:10.895
and things that you've tweeted

00:42:10.895 --> 00:42:14.198
and just the platform in general,
I have to say this. So,

00:42:14.865 --> 00:42:17.401
I remember so vividly

00:42:18.002 --> 00:42:20.304
where I was in 2015

00:42:21.171 --> 00:42:24.842
when the emoji battle happened
with DeAndre Jordan.
- Oh.

00:42:25.209 --> 00:42:28.979
- And everybody—
- I was like, "What is she talking about?"

00:42:28.979 --> 00:42:32.783
To me, that was like the
first big NBA Twitter moment,

00:42:32.816 --> 00:42:36.453
cuz just if everyone was talking about it.
I know what I remember about that night,

00:42:36.787 --> 00:42:38.756
but what do you remember about that night?

00:42:38.756 --> 00:42:46.664
- I remember sitting in the lobby
of a hotel in Houston, and

00:42:47.665 --> 00:42:48.132
what made it

00:42:48.132 --> 00:42:51.402
memorable wasn't so much
what was going on with DeAndre

00:42:51.702 --> 00:42:55.573
as much as the guy
who was working in the hotel

00:42:55.573 --> 00:42:58.776
lobby was, you know,

00:42:59.443 --> 00:43:01.979
Bjelica, the guy who played
for the Warriors last year.
- Mmhmm, yeah.

00:43:02.613 --> 00:43:05.182
- His younger brother was, like,

00:43:05.182 --> 00:43:07.685
the 6-foot-8 guy working the lobby.
- [Laughing]

00:43:08.385 --> 00:43:10.955
- And I'm like, that is just the weirdest thing ever that

00:43:10.955 --> 00:43:13.791
this guy's, you know.
And sitting there with Dan Fegan—

00:43:14.491 --> 00:43:16.827
rest in peace, Dan—and

00:43:17.127 --> 00:43:18.395
wondering what the hell is going on.

00:43:18.395 --> 00:43:20.631
And Dan was his agent. And there was also

00:43:21.732 --> 00:43:24.101
DeAndre, some guy named Steve
something that was there.

00:43:24.101 --> 00:43:26.570
And we couldn't figure out
what was going on.
- Mmhmm.
- And that was it.

00:43:26.570 --> 00:43:29.306
No driving around,
none of that stuff,
- Yeah.
- right?

00:43:29.306 --> 00:43:31.542
And it was just like,
we can't get a hold of him, you know?

00:43:31.542 --> 00:43:34.111
Obviously something's changed,
and moving on.

00:43:34.178 --> 00:43:37.681
- Yeah, cuz I'm like, well,
how does that legend begin,

00:43:37.681 --> 00:43:37.915
right?

00:43:37.915 --> 00:43:38.649
Everyone's like,

00:43:38.649 --> 00:43:41.619
Mark Cuban's driving around
downtown, begging for his address.

00:43:41.852 --> 00:43:43.754
But now it's, like, a thing
that has stuck with you,

00:43:43.754 --> 00:43:46.390
even though you have said,
"This isn't what happened."
- Yeah, yeah.

00:43:46.390 --> 00:43:49.627
But, like, when you started saying 2015,
it didn't even register that that was what you
were going to bring up.

00:43:49.693 --> 00:43:51.328
- Yeah, you're like, "What?"
Yeah.

00:43:51.328 --> 00:43:54.231
- So yeah, that's,
you know, under the bridge.

00:43:54.231 --> 00:43:58.268
But yeah, at the time it was like,
oh, my goodness, this is insane.

00:43:58.302 --> 00:43:58.769
- Yeah.

00:43:58.769 --> 00:44:02.640
- You know, and I was pissed,
but you gotta move on, right?

00:44:03.007 --> 00:44:05.876
It's a business,
- Yeah.
- you know, and players
get to make their choices.

00:44:05.876 --> 00:44:08.912
And I understand why the Clippers did it
because it would have had a huge impact

00:44:08.912 --> 00:44:11.582
on their team.
- Yeah, absolutely.
I know, cuz I'm like, OK,

00:44:11.582 --> 00:44:13.784
so it was just, like, a clubhouse
in his house,

00:44:13.784 --> 00:44:14.885
and all these Clippers were here.

